\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{From a data pipeline to a corpus law}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{pretraining}

\p{We formulate the source mixture and cleaning pipeline separately because
they answer different questions. The mixture in \ref{ftip-0016} describes how
raw documents are proposed. The construction in \ref{ftip-0017} determines
which proposals survive and how they are transformed. Together with all
random seeds and thresholds, they induce a cleaned document law
#{Q_{\mathrm{clean}}}.}

\p{Kaplan et al. describe the concrete WebText2 dataset in
\citet{sec. 2.3}{kaplan2020scaling}. Hoffmann et al. report the MassiveText
sources and mixture in \citet{Appendix A, Table A1}{hoffmann2022training}.
We formulate the source law and ordered cleaning operators because neither
source defines the two generic objects above. A finite released corpus is one
realization of such choices, and an undocumented change in them is not
identified by a loss--compute curve.}
